## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

### Performance Anti-Patterns
```typescript
// ❌ Avoid: Complex calculations in templates
template: `
  <div *ngFor="let item of items">
    {{ expensiveCalculation(item) }}
  </div>
`

// ❌ Avoid: Manual subscription management without cleanup
ngOnInit() {
  this.dataService.getData().subscribe(data => {
    this.data = data; // Memory leak potential
  });
}

// ❌ Avoid: Unnecessary change detection triggers
ngOnInit() {
  setInterval(() => {
    this.cdr.detectChanges(); // Unnecessary change detection
  }, 1000);
}

// ❌ Avoid: Large bundle imports
import * as rxjs from 'rxjs'; // Import entire library
import * as forms from '@angular/forms'; // Import entire module
```

### Memory Anti-Patterns
```typescript
// ❌ Avoid: Event listeners without cleanup
ngOnInit() {
  window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
    this.handleResize();
  }); // No cleanup
}

// ❌ Avoid: Circular references
class ComponentA {
  componentB: ComponentB;
}

class ComponentB {
  componentA: ComponentA; // Circular reference
}

// ❌ Avoid: Large objects in memory
class BadComponent {
  largeData = new Array(1000000).fill('data'); // Large array in memory
}
```

## Best Practices Summary

### Do's ✅
- Use OnPush change detection strategy
- Implement proper subscription cleanup
- Use async pipe when possible
- Implement lazy loading for modules
- Use standalone components
- Optimize bundle size with tree shaking
- Implement caching strategies
- Use virtual scrolling for large lists
- Debounce user inputs
- Monitor performance metrics
- Optimize images and assets
- Use trackBy functions in *ngFor

### Don'ts ❌
- Perform complex calculations in templates
- Forget to clean up subscriptions
- Import entire libraries unnecessarily
- Use manual change detection unnecessarily
- Create memory leaks with event listeners
- Load large datasets without pagination
- Ignore bundle size optimization
- Use inefficient change detection strategies
- Forget to implement proper error handling
- Over-optimize prematurely

